      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_CHE_2679365D_CONCEPTS_01_SCIENTIFIC_NOTATION_FORMAT",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_SCIENTIFIC_NOTATION_FORMAT",
          "title": "1. Scientific Notation Format",
          "contentMarkdown": "In chemistry, extremely large or small numbers are expressed in **scientific notation** as $N \\times 10^n$, where $N$ (the digit term) is a number between $1.000...$ and $9.999...$ and $n$ is an integer exponent (positive or negative). For example, the mass of a hydrogen atom is $1.66 \\times 10^{-24}\\text{ g}$.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_CHE_2679365D_F5D02480"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_CHE_2679365D_CONCEPTS_02_EXPONENTIAL_ARITHMETIC",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_EXPONENTIAL_ARITHMETIC",
          "title": "2. Exponential Arithmetic Rules",
          "contentMarkdown": "In exponential arithmetic:\n- **Multiplication**: exponents are added: $(N_1 \\times 10^{n_1}) \\times (N_2 \\times 10^{n_2}) = (N_1 \\cdot N_2) \\times 10^{n_1 + n_2}$.\n- **Division**: exponents are subtracted: $\\frac{N_1 \\times 10^{n_1}}{N_2 \\times 10^{n_2}} = \\left(\\frac{N_1}{N_2}\\right) \\times 10^{n_1 - n_2}$.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_CHE_2679365D_70D726B8"
          ]
        }
      ],
